From 0e9289ea8596d642b0534af80ef8e4075e1bfb6a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Techniix <Techniix@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 13:13:25 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] nvkmd/nvrm: don't reuse sync backing while queued GPU work
 may use it

The sync type has no WAIT_BEFORE_SIGNAL, so the runtime runs the queue in
THREADED_ON_DEMAND mode, and the first submit that waits on a timeline
point not yet submitted switches the queue to a submit thread for good.
From then on vk_queue_submit_move_binary_waits_to_temps() moves every
binary-semaphore wait into a temporary vk_sync, and
vk_queue_submit_cleanup() destroys that temporary as soon as the driver
has accepted the submission, not when the GPU has run it.

Two bugs made that hang the channel with no RC error, so everything behind
it (e.g. a timeline release the CPU waits for) never ran. OBS (one GL
context used from several threads, through Zink) hit it within its first
frames as a timeline wait stuck at value N-1 with submit_seq N.

1. Slot reuse. The pooled move swaps slots, so the temporary owns the slot
   the queued host SEM acquire reads. Destroying it returned the slot to
   the pool, and the next allocation took the lowest free slot, that one,
   and zeroed it. If the release had already landed, the acquire then
   waited for a value that never came.

   A freed slot now records, for every channel, the fence by which that
   channel will have executed everything already emitted (its last
   submission, or the next one if its push holds unsubmitted methods), and
   goes back to the pool only once each channel has completed it. A
   dedicated sync's memory reference is dropped the same way, which covers
   temporarily imported payloads the runtime destroys right after submit.
   Channels register when they finish init (failing init if that can't be
   recorded) and unregister once their RM channel is freed. wSeq is now
   published before the push is reset, so a concurrent reader errs late,
   never early. The logic is in nvkmd_nvrm_retire.h, free of driver types
   so it can be tested on its own. The first deferral per device is
   reported on stderr.

2. Stale reset. sync_reset_data() set reset_mark to the completed value.
   The runtime resets a binary semaphore right after submitting its wait,
   usually before the GPU has run the signal it consumes (value k-1,
   submit_seq k), so the semaphore read as signaled, and pending, again
   once that release landed. A later move then passed its WAIT_PENDING on
   that stale state and swapped backings before the real next signal was
   emitted: the release went to the new slot while the moved acquire waited
   on the old one. reset_mark now covers submitted signals as well.
---
